      "seed": 6304,
      "response": "To solve for the value of \\( C \\), we start by using the information given in the problem. We know that the arithmetic mean of \\( A \\), \\( B \\), and \\( C \\) is 10. This can be written as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{A + B + C}{3} = 10\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by 3, we get:\n\n\\[\nA + B + C = 30\n\\]\n\nWe are also given that \\( A \\) is six less than \\( B \\), which can be written as:\n\n\\[\nA = B - 6\n\\]\n\nAdditionally, we know that \\( C \\) is three more than \\( B \\), which can be written as:\n\n\\[\nC = B + 3\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute \\( A \\) and \\( C \\) in terms of \\( B \\) into the equation \\( A + B + C = 30 \\):\n\n\\[\n(B - 6) + B + (B + 3) = 30\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\n\\[\nB - 6 + B + B + 3 = 30\n\\]\n\nCombining like terms, we have:\n\n\\[\n3B - 3 = 30\n\\]\n\nAdding 3 to both sides of the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n3B = 33\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by 3, we find:\n\n\\[\nB = 11\n\\]\n\nNow that we have the value of \\( B \\), we can find the values of \\( A \\) and \\( C \\). Using \\( A = B - 6 \\):\n\n\\[\nA = 11 - 6 = 5\n\\]\n\nAnd using \\( C = B + 3 \\):\n\n\\[\nC = 11 + 3 = 14\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( C \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}\n\\]",
